<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaius Sempronius Tuditanus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Sempronius Tuditanus was a Roman politician and writer who served as consul in 129 BCE. In that year he campaigned in the north-eastern Adriatic against the Iapydes and other peoples, and he celebrated a triumph over them. He also wrote works on Roman history and magistracies, which survive only in fragments.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tuditanus belonged to the plebeian gens Sempronia. His father of the same name was a senator and in 146 BCE sat on the commission of ten that reorganised the political order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> more than once confused the two men and was corrected by his friend Titus Pomponius Atticus in May 45 BCE. The younger Tuditanus probably first appears in 146 BCE as an officer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-mummius-achaicus">Lucius Mummius Achaicus</a> in the war in Greece.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was quaestor in 145 BCE and praetor in 132 BCE, probably passing through the offices without difficulty as an adherent of the Scipiones. As consul in 129 BCE with Manius Aquillius he was charged by the senate with judging the complaints of dispossessed Roman allies whose estates the Gracchian land commission had annexed. He did not carry out the task and went instead to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>, allegedly because war threatened, which also held up further allocations of land.</p><p>The sources disagree about his campaign. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> wrote that he first fought the Iapydes without success and that the defeat was made good by a victory owed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decimus-junius-brutus-callaicus">Decimus Junius Brutus Callaicus</a>, while Appian said that he and Tiberius Pandusa seem to have subjugated them. He marked his victories with a dedication to the river god Timavus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> carrying an inscription in Saturnian verse, of which two fragments were found in 1906, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> quoted an inscription on his statue that gave the river Arsa as the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> praised the elegance of his style. Belonging to the Optimates, he wrote a partisan treatise on Roman constitutional law, the libri magistratuum, in at least thirteen books, dealing with intercalation, the appointment of the tribunes of the plebs and the nundinae; with the De potestatibus of the Gracchan Marcus Junius Congus Gracchanus it was the earliest work of its kind in Roman literature. Some scholars ascribe to him a further history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from its foundation, since fragments on the Aborigines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a> and the books said to be King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius">Numa Pompilius</a>'s fit poorly in a work on law.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gaius Sempronius Tuditanus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Herding and raiding into the Roman province of Italy and on Aquileia brought them into conflict with Rome; a campaign under <b>Sempronius Tuditanus</b> defeated them in 129 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/iapydes">Iapydes</a></li></ul></section>
